WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.370
The Meg has swum up from the murky depths
to chomp on theater screens everywhere after

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.160
a long voyage out of prehistory.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.060
Here's a quick look at how the creature feature
came together, how it ends, and why there

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.120
might be a follow-up.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.700
Massive spoilers ahead!

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.600
Based on Steve Alten's 1997 book Meg: A Novel
of Deep Terror, The Meg has been 20 years

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.560
in the making, splashing fruitlessly in development
hell at multiple studios.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.660
At various stages, Guillermo del Toro, Jan
de Bont, and Eli Roth were attached to direct

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.410
before National Treasure's Jon Turteltaub
took the helm and steered the thriller to

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.940
fruition as an American-Chinese co-production.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.350
Even then, it wasn't exactly smooth sailing,
as shifting ratings concerns kept the vision

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:39.680
for the project in flux.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.391
At the heart of the story is rescue diver
Jonas Taylor, played by Jason Statham, who

00:00:44.391 --> 00:00:48.930
joins a group of scientists, including his
ex-wife, on a mission to take down a deadly,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:52.820
supposedly extinct, 75-foot-long Megalodon
shark.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.390
As Statham puts it, it's ...

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.840
"... a fun, shark movie that's like, the ultimate
popcorn movie, a bit of fun that doesn't take

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:01.960
itself too seriously."

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.900
Naturally, The Meg concludes with an escalating
series of confrontations against the prehistoric

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:10.390
beast, including one in which the audience
is briefly led to believe the shark has been

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:11.380
felled with poison.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.560
Alas, the wrong ocean carnivore was killed,
and after a grenade attack launched from a

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.080
helicopter also fails, it falls to our hero
Jonas to save a crowded Chinese beach.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.500
Busting out of his damaged submersible, he
scratches the beast to make it bleed and attract

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.420
other sharks, who devour the Meg, finally
killing it.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.560
While this particular Megalodon definitely
won't be coming back, there is hope for a

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.290
franchise featuring the movie's human characters,
likely based on the original novel's many

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:37.040
follow-ups.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.400
The Meg's secret weapon just might be the
chemistry between its leads.

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:45.930
Jason Statham and Li Bingbing, playing oceanographer
Suyin Zhang, are a likable pair whose romance

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.000
blossoms in the face of shark terror.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.990
Seeing their relationship continue to bloom
while staring down the maw of another deep-sea

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.720
terror could make for a perfectly charming
follow-up.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.200
Then there's "engineering genius" Jaxx Herd,
played by up-and-coming Australian actress

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.080
Ruby Rose.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.500
The Meg is one of Rose's biggest screen roles
yet, after a streak that includes appearances

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:09.229
in Orange Is the New Black, xXx: The Return
of Xander Cage, and John Wick Chapter 2.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:13.480
Just before the release of The Meg, Rose was
announced to be playing Batwoman in the CW's

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:17.779
DC Universe, with the potential for a show
of her very own on the horizon.

00:02:17.779 --> 00:02:21.409
"Yeah, that was probably the most grueling
audition process."

00:02:21.409 --> 00:02:26.309
With a star rising as rapidly as Ruby Rose's,
the idea of Jaxx returning in a Meg sequel

00:02:26.309 --> 00:02:27.860
would likely be very appealing.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.060
But there isn't really any precedence for
studios producing quality shark-attack-based

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.520
sequels, so this is uncharted territory.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:39.180
The original Jaws is an undisputed masterpiece
that changed the face of summer blockbusters

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:39.720
forever.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:45.279
But taken together, Jaws 2, Jaws 3-D, and
Jaws: The Revenge provide one of cinema history's

00:02:45.279 --> 00:02:48.249
most striking examples of the Law of Diminishing
Returns.

00:02:48.249 --> 00:02:50.560
Plus, we're about to get a sixth Sharknado.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.659
So if The Meg gets a sequel, and it's at all
well-received, it would be a first.

00:02:53.659 --> 00:02:58.669
The reviews for The Meg are decidedly mixed,
but early box office projections predict it

00:02:58.669 --> 00:03:03.040
could surpass all financial expectations,
so only time will tell if another journey

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.979
to the bottom of the sea is in Jason Statham's
future.

